{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "title: \"Reproducible Science\"\n",
        "subtitle: \"A Research Memorandum: Driver's License Status and Communication Apprehension\"\n",
        "author: \"JIANGRAN GU\"\n",
        "date: \"2026-07-30\"\n",
        "format:\n",
        "  html:\n",
        "    toc: true\n",
        "    embed-resources: true\n",
        "    code-fold: true\n",
        "execute:\n",
        "  echo: true\n",
        "  warning: false\n",
        "jupyter: python3\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Researcher\n",
        "\n",
        "**Researcher:** JIANGRAN GU\n",
        "**Date:** July 30, 2026\n",
        "**JIANGRAN GU:** Does group communication apprehension predict whether, or how often, respondents use public transportation?\n",
        "**This memo's sub-question:** Does holding a driver's license (Q20) predict communication apprehension (CA)?\n",
        "\n",
        "## Research Or Analytical Question\n",
        "\n",
        "Does whether a respondent **holds a driver's license** (Q20: \"Do you have a license to drive a car?\", Yes/No) predict their level of **communication apprehension (CA)**, measured as the sum of 12 Likert-scale items (Q1–Q6: group-discussion apprehension; Q13–Q18: dyadic/interpersonal apprehension) from the PRCA-style instrument in `PRCAQualtricsExport_FileC.csv`?\n",
        "\n",
        "## Data & Method"
      ],
      "id": "ef2afed5"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: setup\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "from scipy import stats\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "\n",
        "df = pd.read_csv(\"PRCAQualtricsExport_FileC.csv\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# 12 Communication Apprehension items:\n",
        "# Q1-Q6  = group discussion apprehension\n",
        "# Q13-Q18 = dyadic / interpersonal conversation apprehension\n",
        "ca_items = [\"Q1\", \"Q2\", \"Q3\", \"Q4\", \"Q5\", \"Q6\",\n",
        "            \"Q13\", \"Q14\", \"Q15\", \"Q16\", \"Q17\", \"Q18\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "# 5-point ordinal Likert recode (Strongly disagree = 1 ... Strongly agree = 5)\n",
        "likert_map = {\n",
        "    \"Strongly disagree\": 1,\n",
        "    \"Somewhat disagree\": 2,\n",
        "    \"Neither agree nor disagree\": 3,\n",
        "    \"Somewhat agree\": 4,\n",
        "    \"Strongly agree\": 5,\n",
        "}\n",
        "\n",
        "for c in ca_items:\n",
        "    df[c + \"_n\"] = df[c].map(likert_map)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Composite CA score = ordinal sum of all 12 items (per team's agreed coding\n",
        "# scheme -- items are summed as-is with no reverse-scoring)\n",
        "df[\"CA_total\"] = df[[c + \"_n\" for c in ca_items]].sum(axis=1, skipna=False)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Analytic sample: complete CA items + non-missing license status (Q20)\n",
        "sub = df.dropna(subset=[\"CA_total\", \"Q20\"]).copy()\n",
        "sub[\"Q20\"] = pd.Categorical(sub[\"Q20\"], categories=[\"No\", \"Yes\"])\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"Analytic N = {len(sub)}\")\n",
        "print(sub[\"Q20\"].value_counts())"
      ],
      "id": "setup",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "CA items were recoded from text labels to a 1–5 ordinal scale (Strongly disagree = 1 → Strongly agree = 5) and summed across all 12 items to create a composite `CA_total` score (possible range 12–60). Respondents missing any of the 12 CA items or missing Q20 were listwise-deleted, leaving an analytic sample of N = 163 (138 \"Yes\" license holders, 25 \"No\").\n",
        "\n",
        "Group means were compared with an **independent-samples t-test**, with Levene's test used to check the equal-variance assumption.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Answer, Response, + Summary of Results"
      ],
      "id": "db02dd3a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: descriptives\n",
        "desc = sub.groupby(\"Q20\", observed=True)[\"CA_total\"].agg(\n",
        "    n=\"count\", mean=\"mean\", sd=\"std\", median=\"median\"\n",
        ").round(2)\n",
        "desc"
      ],
      "id": "descriptives",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: ttest\n",
        "yes = sub.loc[sub[\"Q20\"] == \"Yes\", \"CA_total\"]\n",
        "no = sub.loc[sub[\"Q20\"] == \"No\", \"CA_total\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "lev_stat, lev_p = stats.levene(yes, no)\n",
        "print(f\"Levene's test: F = {lev_stat:.3f}, p = {lev_p:.3f}\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# Levene's test is non-significant -> equal variances assumed -> Student's t\n",
        "t_stat, p_val = stats.ttest_ind(yes, no, equal_var=True)\n",
        "df_t = len(yes) + len(no) - 2\n",
        "\n",
        "pooled_sd = np.sqrt(\n",
        "    ((len(yes) - 1) * yes.var() + (len(no) - 1) * no.var()) / df_t\n",
        ")\n",
        "cohens_d = (yes.mean() - no.mean()) / pooled_sd\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"t({df_t}) = {t_stat:.3f}, p = {p_val:.3f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Cohen's d = {cohens_d:.3f}\")"
      ],
      "id": "ttest",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| label: fig-ca-by-license\n",
        "#| fig-cap: Communication Apprehension (CA) total score by driver's license status\n",
        "\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6, 4.5))\n",
        "\n",
        "groups = [\"No\", \"Yes\"]\n",
        "data_by_group = [sub.loc[sub[\"Q20\"] == g, \"CA_total\"] for g in groups]\n",
        "\n",
        "bp = ax.boxplot(data_by_group, tick_labels=[f\"{g}\\n(n={len(d)})\" for g, d in zip(groups, data_by_group)],\n",
        "                 patch_artist=True, widths=0.5)\n",
        "for patch, color in zip(bp[\"boxes\"], [\"#f4a582\", \"#92c5de\"]):\n",
        "    patch.set_facecolor(color)\n",
        "\n",
        "# jittered raw points\n",
        "rng = np.random.default_rng(42)\n",
        "for i, d in enumerate(data_by_group, start=1):\n",
        "    x = rng.normal(i, 0.04, size=len(d))\n",
        "    ax.scatter(x, d, alpha=0.4, color=\"black\", s=15)\n",
        "\n",
        "ax.set_xlabel(\"Has a driver's license? (Q20)\")\n",
        "ax.set_ylabel(\"Communication Apprehension total score (12–60)\")\n",
        "ax.set_title(\"CA Total Score by Driver's License Status\")\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fig-ca-by-license",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "raw",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Using `PRCAQualtricsExport_FileC.csv`, respondents **without** a driver's license reported a slightly **higher** mean CA score (M = 37.40, SD = 2.53, n = 25) than respondents **with** a license (M = 36.38, SD = 2.55, n = 138). Levene's test indicated the equal-variance assumption was reasonable (F = 0.02, p = .881), so a Student's independent-samples t-test was used. The difference was **not statistically significant at the conventional α = .05 level**, t(161) = -1.83, p = .069, although it approached significance at the α = .10 level. The effect size was small-to-moderate (Cohen's d = -0.40), with the no-license group trending toward higher apprehension.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Conclusion:** From these data, holding a driver's license does **not** significantly predict communication apprehension at the .05 threshold, though there is a marginal (p < .10), small-to-moderate trend suggesting non-license-holders may report somewhat higher CA. This should be treated as suggestive rather than conclusive evidence.\n",
        "\n",
        "## What questions or uncertainties remain?\n",
        "\n",
        "- The composite `CA_total` was created by **summing raw ordinal codes without reverse-scoring** the positively-worded items (e.g., Q2, Q4, Q6, Q14, Q16, Q17), per the team's agreed coding approach. A standard PRCA-style reverse-scored composite might change the magnitude (and possibly the direction) of the group difference — this is worth checking as a robustness test.\n",
        "- The \"No license\" subgroup is small (n = 25), which limits statistical power; the marginal p-value (.069) could reflect low power as much as a true null effect.\n",
        "- Does license status relate differently to the **group-discussion** (Q1–Q6) vs. **dyadic/interpersonal** (Q13–Q18) CA subscales separately, rather than only the combined total?\n",
        "- How does license status intersect with the team's core question — does CA (or license status) predict actual public-transportation use (Q26–Q27) or ride-share use (Q28–Q29)? A follow-up regression/mediation analysis could connect this sub-question back to the group's shared research question.\n",
        "- Possible confounds (age, car access [Q21], urban vs. rural location) were not controlled for here and could be examined in a multivariate model.\n",
        "\n",
        "---"
      ],
      "id": "9e96cb23"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "*Data source: `PRCAQualtricsExport_FileC.csv`, PRCA-style survey administered as part of Psych 755, University of Wisconsin–Madison. Variable definitions per `data_dictionary.csv`.*"
      ],
      "id": "ee7b112f"
    }
  ],
